I. Foundations & Early History (1950–1980)
Turing Test: A behavioral benchmark proposed by Alan Turing (1950) to determine if a machine can exhibit intelligent behavior indistinguishable from a human.
Dartmouth Workshop: The 1956 summer research project where the term "Artificial Intelligence" was officially coined by John McCarthy.
LISP: The primary programming language for AI research created in 1958, known for its ability to process symbolic information.
Perceptron: The first neural network hardware, built by Frank Rosenblatt in 1957, capable of basic image recognition.
ELIZA: The world’s first chatbot, created by Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT (1966), which simulated a Rogerian psychotherapist.
Unimate: The first industrial robot, deployed by General Motors in 1961 to handle hazardous die-castings on an assembly line.
Shakey the Robot: Developed at SRI International (1966), it was the first mobile robot with the ability to reason about its own actions.
Expert Systems: AI programs from the 1970s that used "If-Then" rules to mimic the decision-making ability of human experts.
AI Winter: Periods (most notably the mid-1970s and late 1980s) where interest and funding for AI collapsed due to over-hyped promises.
Symbolic AI: An approach based on the high-level "symbolic" (human-readable) representations of problems and logic.
II. The Rise of Data (1990–2010)
Deep Blue: The IBM supercomputer that defeated world chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997.
Backpropagation: The fundamental algorithm used to train neural networks by calculating the "error" and adjusting weights backward through layers.
ImageNet: A massive dataset of labeled images launched by Fei-Fei Li (2009) that catalyzed the modern deep learning revolution.
MNIST: A classic dataset of 70,000 handwritten digits used as the "Hello World" of machine learning for testing algorithms.
Support Vector Machines (SVM): A popular supervised learning model used for classification and regression before neural networks became dominant.
Random Forest: An ensemble learning method that operates by constructing a multitude of decision trees during training.
Big Data: The era starting in the 2000s where the sheer volume of web data allowed AI models to "learn" without hand-coded rules.
Netflix Prize: A 2006 competition that accelerated the development of recommendation algorithms.
Roomba: The first commercially successful domestic robot (iRobot, 2002), using basic AI for floor mapping and obstacle avoidance.
Siri: Originally a standalone app acquired by Apple in 2010, marking the first mainstream integration of a virtual assistant into a phone.
III. Deep Learning & The Modern Era (2012–Present)
AlexNet: The neural network that won the 2012 ImageNet challenge by a landslide, proving the power of Deep Learning.
Transformers: The revolutionary architecture introduced in the paper "Attention Is All You Need" (2017) that powers almost all modern LLMs.
GPT (Generative Pre-trained Transformer): A family of language models by OpenAI that use unsupervised learning to generate human-like text.
Large Language Model (LLM): An AI trained on massive text datasets to understand, summarize, and generate language.
AlphaGo: The Google DeepMind AI that defeated the world Go champion Lee Sedol in 2016.
TensorFlow: An open-source library developed by Google for building and training machine learning models.
PyTorch: A competing open-source machine learning framework, primarily developed by Meta’s AI Research lab.
Hugging Face: The central community platform and "library" where researchers share open-source AI models and datasets.
NVIDIA H100: The industry-standard graphics processing unit (GPU) designed specifically to train the world's most powerful AI models.
Generative AI: A subset of AI focused on creating new content (text, images, audio, video) rather than just analyzing existing data.
IV. Tools & Frameworks
API (Application Programming Interface): The "bridge" that allows third-party apps to access the power of models like GPT-4 or Claude.
Prompt Engineering: The practice of refining the text input given to an AI to achieve a more accurate or creative result.
Fine-Tuning: Taking a pre-trained model and training it further on a smaller, specific dataset to make it an expert in one field.
RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): A technique that lets an AI "look up" specific documents in real-time to answer questions accurately.
LangChain: A popular framework for "chaining" different AI tools together to build complex autonomous agents.
Jupyter Notebook: A web-based interactive environment where data scientists write code and visualize AI training data.
Weights & Biases: A tool used by developers to track and visualize the performance of their AI models during training.
Cuda: A parallel computing platform by NVIDIA that allows AI researchers to use GPUs for general-purpose processing.
Llama: Meta’s family of open-weight LLMs that allowed individual developers to run powerful AI on their own hardware.
Stable Diffusion: An open-source latent text-to-image diffusion model that can be run locally on consumer PCs.
V. Core Technical Terms
Neural Network: A series of algorithms that endeavors to recognize underlying relationships in a set of data through a process that mimics the human brain.
Token: The basic unit of text (often a word or part of a word) that an LLM processes.
Context Window: The "memory limit" of an AI—how much text it can "read" and remember at one time during a single session.
Inference: The process of a trained AI model actually providing an answer or generating an output for a user.
Hallucination: When an AI model generates information that sounds plausible but is factually incorrect or nonsensical.
Alignment: The ongoing research challenge of ensuring that an AI’s goals and behaviors match human values and safety standards.
Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF): A training method where humans rank AI responses to help it become more helpful and less harmful.
Parameters: The internal "variables" within a model that it adjusts during training; generally, more parameters mean a more complex "brain."
Multimodal: An AI that can process and output multiple types of data, such as seeing an image and describing it in text.
Latent Space: A mathematical "map" where the AI stores the relationship between different concepts or images.
